@meta
id: 22-19
title: Scenario — the currency rollout
chapter: 22
type: scenario
skills: visual-block, block-insert, block-append, open-o, buffer-switch
par: 96
minutes: 12
vimrc: set hidden
vscode: three tabs, a lot of Alt+clicking, and a regex for the CSV
open: currency.ts order.entity.ts orders.csv errors.json

@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    CUR-201  Orders settle in more than one currency from next month.
             The entity, the currency constants and the reconcile
             fixture all need to know.

Four files, open as four buffers. `:bn` moves to the next; `:wqa`
writes and quits the lot at the end.

  1. currency.ts — the five currency constants are only used inside
     the file today. The provider needs them: export all five. The
     comment and the `type` line stay as they are.

  2. order.entity.ts — `Order` gets a `currency: Currency;` field,
     directly under `total`.

  3. orders.csv — the reconcile fixture needs a `currency` column at
     the end. Every one of the thirty data rows gets `,GBP`; the header
     gets `,currency`.

  4. errors.json — nothing to do. It is open because the ticket
     mentions it, and to prove you can leave a file alone.

@keys
| `Ctrl-V` then `I` | insert at the left edge of a block, every line |
| `Ctrl-V` then `$A` | append at the end of every line in the block |
| `G` | in block mode, extend the block to the last line |
| `o` | open a line below and insert |
| `:bn` `:wqa` | next buffer; write all and quit |

@hints
The constants are five consecutive lines starting at the same column.
That is a block: `Ctrl-V`, down four, `I`, the word, `Esc`.
--
For the CSV, do not count rows. From the first data row, `Ctrl-V`
then `G` reaches the last, `$` makes the block ragged, `A` appends.
The header is one line and gets an ordinary `A`.
--
currency.ts: `/const` `Enter`, `Ctrl-V`, `4j`, `Iexport `, `Esc`.
order.entity.ts: `/total` `Enter`, `o`, type the field, `Esc`.
orders.csv: `j`, `Ctrl-V`, `G`, `$`, `A,GBP`, `Esc`, `gg`,
`A,currency`, `Esc`. `:wqa`.

@solution
A route, buffer by buffer.

**currency.ts** — `/const` `Enter`, `Ctrl-V`, `4j`, `Iexport `, `Esc`.
`:bn`.

**order.entity.ts** — `/total` `Enter`, `o`, `currency: Currency;`,
`Esc`. `:bn`.

**orders.csv** — `j` past the header, `Ctrl-V`, `G`, `$`, `A,GBP`,
`Esc`. Then `gg`, `A,currency`, `Esc`. `:wqa`.

**errors.json** — untouched.

Around eighty-three keystrokes, thirty of them typed text. The two
block edits are the point: five lines or five hundred, the keys are
the same, and neither needed a count that could be off by one. The
`G` in the CSV is the habit to keep — a block is extended by motions,
and the motion that says "to the end of the file" is always three
keys shorter than counting.

@start currency.ts
// Supported settlement currencies. ISO 4217, upper case.

const GBP = 'GBP';
const EUR = 'EUR';
const USD = 'USD';
const CHF = 'CHF';
const SEK = 'SEK';

export type Currency = typeof GBP | typeof EUR | typeof USD | typeof CHF | typeof SEK;

@expected currency.ts
// Supported settlement currencies. ISO 4217, upper case.

export const GBP = 'GBP';
export const EUR = 'EUR';
export const USD = 'USD';
export const CHF = 'CHF';
export const SEK = 'SEK';

export type Currency = typeof GBP | typeof EUR | typeof USD | typeof CHF | typeof SEK;

@start order.entity.ts
import { Currency } from './currency';

export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
import { Currency } from './currency';

export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  currency: Currency;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

